title: JushiD2_FoShou

position: 1536,687

---

Hou: Oh, there's one with Buddha's hand fruit! That little lid knob is rather cute.

Me: Buddha's hand is a kind of citron, a citrus fruit shaped like outstretched fingers.

Me: Traditionally it's auspicious because "Buddha" and "good fortune" sound alike in Chinese.

Me: It's made from the same clay as the Longevity Peach. Both add decorations to an established teapot form.

(You take out the lenticular card and hand it to Hou.

As she compares and handles it, you turn toward the display cabinet, scanning the shelves for the actual teapot.)

//Pause the dialogue panel here; press Space to show it again.

<<pause_dialogue>>

(Found it! You place the teapot before Hou.)

Me: But this one's an overhead-handle teapot, not a basic round design.

(Hou runs her fingers over the teapot.)

Me: If you choose this one, it'll be quite different from the Gongchun you bought last time.

Hou: Good fortune, good connections. That's a lovely meaning.

Hou: (She presses her lips together.) You know, a person's good fortune isn't limited to one lifetime.

Me: Hmm?

Hou: You see, we're choosing a teapot because we've acquired the taste for it, and because we have enough of... this—

(She gestures as if counting money, then smiles gently.)

Hou: Once sages reach a certain level of attainment, they can choose where they come into the world.

Hou: Truly. If you've attained enlightenment, you can decide where you'll be reborn, and who your parents will be.

Hou: You see, people choose to enter through their mother's egg, because entering through the father's sperm is far too unreliable.

Hou: A man releases tens of thousands of sperm at once. For just one to join the single egg, the odds are terribly slim.

Hou: And so many are aborted before they're even fully formed. A woman has only so many eggs in her lifetime. That's why a human birth is so precious.

Me: Mm, right. Every child is born from a womb.

Hou: Don't think I'm making this up.

Hou: There's science behind it. The continuity of life is, after all, the transmission of information.

Hou: Ordinary minds contain seeds of all kinds, wandering among the five realms of existence.

Hou: What we call attaining sainthood is really the unification of one's mind.

Hou: A sage has one great seed of mind, whereas our rebirths are determined by the karma that propels us and the karma that completes our circumstances.

Hou: As the seed of sagehood grows, you can take charge of your own life, perhaps even in the next one.

Me: Oh. Well, I'm grateful to have been born human in this life.

[You hear Ke Xiang and Baochai calling.]

Hou: Yes.

(She looks once more at the Buddha's Hand teapot and presses her lips together.)

<<jump JushiD2_FoShouConfirm>>

===



