Me: Look, it's quite different from those round teapots.

(You turn the catalog to page two and lay it open before Hou.)

//Pause the dialogue panel here; press Space to show it again.

<<pause_dialogue>>

Hou: Isn't it just square?

Me: A common way to make a zisha teapot is to paddle the clay into a cylindrical body.

Me: You take a uniformly flattened slab of clay, wrap it around a circular base, and paddle it into a rounded body.

(As you speak, you take out a lenticular card and hand it to Hou.

While she handles it and compares the images, you turn toward the display cabinet and scan the shelves for the teapot.)

//Pause the dialogue panel here; press Space to show it again.

<<pause_dialogue>>

(Found it! You place it before Hou.)

Me: Square teapots are often made by joining slabs. You cut each slab first, assemble them, and refine the edges.

Me: Here, only once the corners have been joined does it become a complete body.

Me: I think, as a gift for the two young people, this could suggest that

Me: two people who once led separate lives will have to learn how to fit together and connect.

<<jump JushiD2_FangPianConfirm>>

